#
# Vendor: The Apache Software Foundation
# Product web page: https://airflow.apache.org
# Affected version: 3.3.2
#
# Summary: Apache Airflow is an open-source platform for developing,
# scheduling, and monitoring workflows, such as traditional time based
# or event-triggered batch-oriented data pipelines, machine learning,
# model training, and agentic or LLM-based workloads. Airflow's extensible
# Python framework enables you to build workflows connecting with virtually
# any technology, with a growing set of providers for orchestrating AI
# and agentic tools alongside the rest of your pipeline. A web-based UI
# helps you visualize, manage, and debug your workflows. You can run Airflow
# in a variety of configurations - from a single process on your laptop
# to a distributed system capable of handling massive workloads.
#
# Desc: Apache Airflow suffers from a sensitive information disclosure
# vulnerability caused by an incomplete secret-masking routine. In the
# secrets masker, container structures are walked without a depth limit
# for key-name redaction, but pattern-based masking of registered secrets
# is applied only to string values at or below a fixed recursion depth
# (MAX_RECURSION_DEPTH): beyond that depth the scalar is returned as-is,
# before the secret-replacement step runs. As a result, a configured secret
# that appears as a string value nested under non-sensitive keys several
# container levels deep is not redacted and is emitted in clear text to
# task logs. An operator, or anyone with log access, can recover credentials
# that were expected to be masked.
#
# ========================================================================
#     depth 0: masked (***)
#     ...
#     depth 5: masked (***)
#     depth 6: LEAK (cleartext)
#     ...
# [+] depth-6 result (as stored/shown in Rendered Templates / task logs):
#     {'op_kwargs': {'a': {'b': {'c': {'d': {'e': {'l6': 'S3cr3t-Pa55word-y0'}}}}}}}
# [+] LEAK CONFIRMED - mask_secret() value returned in cleartext past depth 5.
# ========================================================================
#
# Tested on: GNU/Linux x86_64
#            Python 3.10+
